---
import LegalPage from '~/layouts/LegalPage.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL as CONTACT } from '~/lib/contact';
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Taxonomap stores, what it sends to other services, and your rights."
  updated="31 October 2026"
>
  <p>
    Taxonomap is a free, non-commercial explorer for biodiversity records. It has no accounts, no cookies, no
    advertising and no analytics or tracking. This page explains the little that does happen to your data.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p>
    Taxonomap is run by the Taxonomap project, which is the data controller for this site. Questions or requests about
    your data go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we receive</h2>
  <p>
    The site is static: its pages are built in advance and served by <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, our hosting provider.
    As with any website, Cloudflare receives your IP address, browser details and the pages you request so it can
    deliver them and protect the site from abuse. It keeps these logs briefly under its own{' '}
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">
      privacy policy
    </a>
    . We don’t combine them with anything or use them to identify you.
  </p>
  <p>We have no database, so there is nothing about you stored on our side.</p>

  <h2>What stays in your browser</h2>
  <p>
    A few settings you choose are saved in your browser’s local storage, so they are still there next time. They never
    leave your device, and you can clear them at any time in your browser’s settings.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Theme</strong>: light, dark or automatic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map colours</strong>: the colour ramp you picked for the map.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Getting started</strong>: whether you’ve seen the tour, and your progress through “Try this”.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Request meter</strong>: whether you’ve hidden it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photo lookups</strong>: which iNaturalist entry matches which species, so photos load faster.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A service worker also keeps copies of map tiles, photos and data you’ve viewed, so the site is quicker, and a page
    to show when you’re offline. These copies contain no personal data. Because all of this is only what the site needs
    to do what you ask, it doesn’t need a consent banner.
  </p>

  <h2>Services your browser talks to</h2>
  <p>
    To show maps, records and photos, your browser fetches them straight from the services below. Each one receives your
    IP address and the request itself (for example, which species or map area you’re looking at), and handles it under
    its own privacy policy.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>GBIF</strong> (Global Biodiversity Information Facility): occurrence records, species information, map
      density tiles and record photos, from its REST API (api.gbif.org) and its GraphQL API (graphql.gbif.org).{' '}
      <a href="https://www.gbif.org/terms/privacy-policy" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>iNaturalist</strong>: extra species photos, loaded from iNaturalist’s photo storage on Amazon Web
      Services.{' '}
      <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/pages/privacy" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wikimedia</strong> (Wikidata and Wikipedia): the introduction on a species page, for species without a
      pre-built page. The requests name only the species’ GBIF number and the Wikidata item and article it leads to.{' '}
      <a href="https://foundation.wikimedia.org/wiki/Policy:Privacy_policy" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenFreeMap</strong>: the plain basemap.{' '}
      <a href="https://openfreemap.org/privacy/" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>EOX IT Services</strong>: the satellite basemap, only when you switch to it.{' '}
      <a href="https://eox.at/privacy-policy/" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photon</strong>, run by komoot: place search. Only what you type into a place search is sent, as you type
      it.{' '}
      <a href="https://www.komoot.com/privacy" rel="noopener">
        Privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Fonts are served from our own site, not from Google or another font service.</p>

  <h2>Your location</h2>
  <p>
    “What’s near me” can use your device’s location, but only if you press the button and your browser asks you and you
    agree. The location is rounded to about 100 metres before it is used. It is then:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>sent to GBIF to find records nearby, and implied by the map tiles your browser loads around it;</li>
    <li>
      written into the page address (<code>?at=</code>) so the page can be bookmarked.{' '}
      <strong>If you share that link, you share that rounded location.</strong>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>It is not stored anywhere else. You can pick a place on the map or search for one instead.</p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under UK and EU data protection law (UK GDPR and GDPR) you can ask what personal data we hold about you, and ask us
    to correct or delete it. In practice we hold none outside Cloudflare’s short-lived logs. Where we rely on our
    legitimate interest in running a secure, working site, you can object. Email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. If you’re unhappy with our answer, you can complain to the{' '}
    <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="noopener">
      Information Commissioner’s Office
    </a>{' '}
    or your local data protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If what the site does with data changes, this page changes first, with a new date at the top. See also the{' '}
    <a href="/terms/">terms and credits</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
